      "knowledge_point_id": "cee807c1-dd86-5b0e-a595-5c5754c47e4c",
      "knowledge_point": "Computing Covariance in Context",
      "problem": "| **Age, in years ($x$)** | $12$ | $12$ | $14$ | $14$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Weight, in lbs ($y$)** | $85$ | $90$ | $97$ | $100$ |\n\nMike plays baseball. The table above shows the ages and corresponding weights of four of Mike's teammates.\nCompute the covariance of the data sets.",
      "worked_solution": "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the means: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (12+12+14+14) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 52 \\\\  & =13 \\\\ \\overset{―}{y} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (85+90+97+100) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 372 \\\\  & =93\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(12\\,-\\,13)(85\\,-\\,93)+(12\\,-\\,13)(90\\,-\\,93)+(14\\,-\\,13)(97\\,-\\,93)+(14\\,-\\,13)(100\\,-\\,93)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-1)(-8)+(-1)(-3)+(1)(4)+(1)(7)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[8+3+4+7] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 22 \\\\  & =5.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
